Hi Palantir.net team,

We sent you a client security audit for uchicago.edu with a published grade o=
f F (40/100). That grade was wrong. Our scoring model at the time penalised e=
very finding flatly and counted best-practice configuration items as vulnerab=
ilities. Re-scoring the same scan under the corrected model gives B (80/100).

Three finding classes drove the wrong grade: the DMARC policy in inactive mon=
itoring mode (p=3Dnone), the missing HTTP response hardening headers (CSP, X-=
Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, C=
OOP) and the missing mail security records (BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT).

That scan returned no critical or high finding, so the published failure did =
not describe exploit risk.

The rule now: best-practice items are reported but never scored, a scan with =
no critical or high finding cannot score below 60, and repeated findings of t=
he same class are charged at a reduced rate under a per-class cap.

Next step: reply to this note and we will re-issue the uchicago.edu audit wit=
h the corrected grade and score. No change to the client's configuration is r=
equired to correct the score.

Best regards,
Carson Haynes
